{"title":"Learning to Classify Email into “Speech Acts”","url":"https://aclanthology.org/W04-3240/","authors":["William W. Cohen","Vitor R. Carvalho","Tom M. Mitchell"],"year":2004,"abstract":"Presents supervised methods for classifying email according to sender intent using an ontology containing communicative acts such as proposing, committing, delivering, and requesting. The work is motivated by tracking the status of ongoing joint activities in workplace email.","venue":"Proceedings of the 2004 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing"}
{"title":"Email Task Management: An Iterative Relational Learning Approach","url":"https://dblp.org/rec/conf/ceas/KhoussainovK05","authors":["Rinat Khoussainov","Nicholas Kushmerick"],"year":2005,"abstract":"Studies tasks and activities distributed over multiple email messages and jointly identifies task-related messages and semantic relations between them. Its semantic analysis includes communicative categories such as Propose, Request, Deliver, Commit, and Amend, and is evaluated on real-world email corpora.","venue":"Second Conference on Email and Anti-Spam"}
{"title":"On the collective classification of email \"speech acts\"","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1076034.1076094","doi":"10.1145/1076034.1076094","authors":["Vitor R. Carvalho","William W. Cohen"],"year":2005,"abstract":"Classifies email messages for communicative acts such as requests and commitments while exploiting dependencies among messages in the same thread. A dependency-network approach performs collective classification using relational features derived from neighboring messages.","venue":"Proceedings of the 28th Annual International ACM SIGIR Conference on Research and Development in Information Retrieval"}
{"title":"Classifying speech acts using verbal response modes","url":"https://researchers.mq.edu.au/en/publications/classifying-speech-acts-using-verbal-response-modes/","authors":["Andrew Lampert","Robert Dale","Cecile Paris"],"year":2006,"abstract":"Investigates statistical classification of utterance-level dialogic functions in email using the Verbal Response Modes taxonomy. The stated application is automated assistance for understanding the status of email conversations and connecting speech acts across messages.","venue":"Proceedings of the 2006 Australasian Language Technology Workshop"}
{"title":"DREAM: Classification Scheme for Dialog Acts in Clinical Research Query Mediation","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbi.2015.11.011","doi":"10.1016/j.jbi.2015.11.011","authors":["Julia Hoxha","Praveen Chandar","Zhe He","James Cimino","David Hanauer","Chunhua Weng"],"year":2016,"published":"2015-11-30","abstract":"Develops and empirically validates a dialog-act annotation scheme using email exchanges for clinical research data requests. The corpus contains task-oriented multi-message dialogues, and the taxonomy includes task-management and resolution states as well as backward-looking correction or suggestion acts.","venue":"Journal of Biomedical Informatics"}
{"title":"LEDA: a Large-Organization Email-Based Decision-Dialogue-Act Analysis Dataset","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-acl.378/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2023.findings-acl.378","authors":["Vanja Mladen Karan","Prashant Khare","Ravi Shekhar","Stephen McQuistin","Ignacio Castro","Gareth Tyson","Colin Perkins","Patrick Healey","Matthew Purver"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Introduces a dialog-act dataset derived from real-world IETF mailing-list discussions about organizational decision making and trains transformer-based dialog-act classifiers. The annotation inventory includes action proposals, stated decisions, agreement, disagreement, information exchange, and clarification-related acts, and the analysis examines communication over the life cycle of decision discussions.","venue":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2023"}
{"title":"User Edits Classification Using Document Revision Histories","url":"https://aclanthology.org/E12-1036/","authors":["Amit Bronner","Christof Monz"],"year":2012,"abstract":"Uses supervised learning over document revision histories to distinguish factual edits from fluency-oriented edits. Features include language-model probabilities, string similarity, part-of-speech information, named entities, and adaptive revision-history features.","venue":"Proceedings of the 13th Conference of the European Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"Automatically Classifying Edit Categories in Wikipedia Revisions","url":"https://aclanthology.org/D13-1055/","doi":"10.18653/v1/D13-1055","authors":["Johannes Daxenberger","Iryna Gurevych"],"year":2013,"venue":"Proceedings of the 2013 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing"}
{"title":"Edit Categories and Editor Role Identification in Wikipedia","url":"https://aclanthology.org/L16-1206/","authors":["Diyi Yang","Aaron Halfaker","Robert Kraut","Eduard Hovy"],"year":2016,"abstract":"Presents an annotated corpus of Wikipedia revisions with fine-grained edit categories and machine-learning models for automatically identifying those categories. The study also models editor roles from patterns of editing behavior across revisions.","venue":"Proceedings of the Tenth International Conference on Language Resources and Evaluation"}
{"title":"Identifying Semantic Edit Intentions from Revisions in Wikipedia","url":"https://aclanthology.org/D17-1213/","doi":"10.18653/v1/D17-1213","authors":["Diyi Yang","Aaron Halfaker","Robert Kraut","Eduard Hovy"],"year":2017,"abstract":"Defines a fine-grained taxonomy of semantic edit intentions in Wikipedia revisions, including intentions such as clarification, elaboration, factual updating, and verification. It annotates edits with these intentions and evaluates automatic classification models.","venue":"Proceedings of the 2017 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing"}
{"title":"One Document, Many Revisions: A Dataset for Classification and Description of Edit Intents","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.lrec-1.591/","authors":["Dheeraj Rajagopal","Xuchao Zhang","Michael Gamon","Sujay Kumar Jauhar","Diyi Yang","Eduard Hovy"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Introduces a dataset based on complete Wikipedia revision histories with edit-intent labels associated with individual revisions. The paper evaluates edit-intent classification and generation of natural-language descriptions of revisions, explicitly modeling iterative revision histories rather than isolated document pairs.","venue":"Proceedings of the Thirteenth Language Resources and Evaluation Conference"}
{"title":"Understanding Iterative Revision from Human-Written Text","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-long.250/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2022.acl-long.250","authors":["Wanyu Du","Vipul Raheja","Dhruv Kumar","Zae Myung Kim","Melissa Lopez","Dongyeop Kang"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Studies sequences of human-written revisions and introduces a corpus annotated with edit intentions across multiple domains. The work evaluates models that use distinctions among edit intentions to better understand and perform iterative text revision.","venue":"Proceedings of the 60th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"Improving Iterative Text Revision by Learning Where to Edit from Other Revision Tasks","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.01350","arxiv_id":"2212.01350","doi":"10.18653/v1/2022.emnlp-main.678","authors":["Zae Myung Kim","Wanyu Du","Vipul Raheja","Dhruv Kumar","Dongyeop Kang"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Models iterative text revision as a process that detects editable spans, predicts edit intentions, and generates revised text. It transfers supervision from related revision datasets to improve identification of where and how a document should be revised.","venue":"Proceedings of the 2022 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing"}
{"title":"arXivEdits: Understanding the Human Revision Process in Scientific Writing","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.15067","arxiv_id":"2210.15067","doi":"10.18653/v1/2022.emnlp-main.641","authors":["Chao Jiang","Wei Xu","Samuel Stevens"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Constructs a corpus of scientific papers with multiple arXiv versions, aligns content across versions, and annotates fine-grained span edits and edit intentions. The work evaluates document, sentence, and word alignment together with automatic edit-intention classification.","venue":"Proceedings of the 2022 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing"}
{"title":"Get Your Vitamin C! Robust Fact Verification with Contrastive Evidence","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08541","arxiv_id":"2103.08541","doi":"10.18653/v1/2021.naacl-main.52","authors":["Tal Schuster","Adam Fisch","Regina Barzilay"],"year":2021,"abstract":"Introduces contrastive evidence derived from Wikipedia revisions in which small factual changes can alter whether evidence supports or refutes the same claim. The work explicitly treats evidence as changing over time and evaluates factual-revision detection and robust fact verification.","venue":"Proceedings of the 2021 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies"}
{"title":"FacTA: Evaluation of Event Factuality and Temporal Anchoring","url":"https://books.openedition.org/aaccademia/13703","doi":"10.4000/books.accademia.1509","authors":["Anne-Lyse Myriam Minard","Manuela Speranza","Rachele Sprugnoli","Tommaso Caselli"],"year":2015,"abstract":"Evaluates event factuality profiling together with temporal anchoring. Factuality is represented relative to the main source through dimensions including certainty, polarity, and temporal status, while temporal annotations connect events to temporal anchors such as document creation time and explicit temporal expressions.","venue":"Second Italian Conference on Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"MEANTIME, the NewsReader Multilingual Event and Time Corpus","url":"https://aclanthology.org/L16-1699/","authors":["Anne-Lyse Minard","Manuela Speranza","Ruben Urizar","Begoña Altuna","Marieke van Erp","Anneleen Schoen","Chantal van Son"],"year":2016,"abstract":"Introduces a multilingual corpus annotated for entities, events, temporal expressions, semantic roles, temporal relations, and event and entity coreference, including cross-document links. The resource supports research combining event identity and temporal organization across documents.","venue":"Proceedings of the Tenth International Conference on Language Resources and Evaluation"}
{"title":"Neural Models of Factuality","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.02472","arxiv_id":"1804.02472","doi":"10.18653/v1/N18-1067","authors":["Rachel Rudinger","Aaron Steven White","Benjamin Van Durme"],"year":2018,"abstract":"Develops neural models for predicting the factuality of events described in text and evaluates them on multiple factuality datasets. The models estimate source-relative textual commitment rather than independently verified truth.","venue":"Proceedings of the 2018 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies"}
{"title":"Graph based Neural Networks for Event Factuality Prediction using Syntactic and Semantic Structures","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P19-1432/","doi":"10.18653/v1/P19-1432","authors":["Amir Pouran Ben Veyseh","Thien Huu Nguyen","Dejing Dou"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Studies event factuality prediction with graph neural networks that combine syntactic and semantic structures. The task predicts the degree to which an event is presented as factual in its textual context and provides a transferable component for source-relative event-state modeling.","venue":"Proceedings of the 57th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"FEVER: a Large-scale Dataset for Fact Extraction and VERification","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.05355","arxiv_id":"1803.05355","doi":"10.18653/v1/N18-1074","authors":["James Thorne","Andreas Vlachos","Christos Christodoulopoulos","Arpit Mittal"],"year":2018,"abstract":"Introduces a large fact-verification dataset containing claims labeled Supported, Refuted, or NotEnoughInfo together with evidence sentences from Wikipedia. It provides an empirical framework for separating an asserted claim from independently retrieved textual evidence used to corroborate or refute it.","venue":"Proceedings of the 2018 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies"}
{"title":"DeClarE: Debunking Fake News and False Claims using Evidence-Aware Deep Learning","url":"https://aclanthology.org/D18-1003/","doi":"10.18653/v1/D18-1003","authors":["Kashyap Popat","Subhabrata Mukherjee","Andrew Yates","Gerhard Weikum"],"year":2018,"abstract":"Presents an evidence-aware neural model for assessing claim credibility using external evidence articles, textual signals, and information about source trustworthiness. The approach explicitly separates claims from supporting or contradicting evidence gathered from other sources.","venue":"Proceedings of the 2018 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing"}
{"title":"Evidence-based Trustworthiness","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P19-1040/","doi":"10.18653/v1/P19-1040","authors":["Yi Zhang","Zachary Ives","Dan Roth"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Develops probabilistic models that jointly estimate source trustworthiness and claim credibility when multiple sources make assertions and provide evidence. The framework distinguishes directly asserted claims from claims inferred through noisy NLP processing and evaluates source- and evidence-aware credibility estimation.","venue":"Proceedings of the 57th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"MultiFC: A Real-World Multi-Domain Dataset for Evidence-Based Fact Checking of Claims","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.03242","arxiv_id":"1909.03242","doi":"10.18653/v1/D19-1475","authors":["Isabelle Augenstein","Christina Lioma","Dongsheng Wang","Lucas Chaves Lima","Casper Hansen","Christian Hansen","Jakob Grue Simonsen"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Introduces a multi-domain collection of real-world claims from fact-checking organizations together with textual evidence sources, metadata, and expert veracity judgments. The work evaluates models that jointly rank evidence pages and predict claim veracity.","venue":"Proceedings of the 2019 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing and the 9th International Joint Conference on Natural Language Processing"}
{"title":"“Who said it, and Why?” Provenance for Natural Language Claims","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.406/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2020.acl-main.406","authors":["Yi Zhang","Zachary Ives","Dan Roth"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Introduces a provenance representation for natural-language claims that records where a claim originated and how it evolved. The approach builds provenance graphs using information extraction and textual entailment and evaluates provenance inference together with claim verification.","venue":"Proceedings of the 58th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"Fact or Fiction: Verifying Scientific Claims","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.14974","arxiv_id":"2004.14974","doi":"10.18653/v1/2020.emnlp-main.609","authors":["David Wadden","Shanchuan Lin","Kyle Lo","Lucy Lu Wang","Madeleine van Zuylen","Arman Cohan","Hannaneh Hajishirzi"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Introduces SciFact, a dataset of scientific claims paired with abstracts containing evidence that supports or refutes them and annotated rationales. The work evaluates retrieval and reasoning models that verify claims against independently selected scientific evidence.","venue":"Proceedings of the 2020 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing"}
{"title":"FEVEROUS: Fact Extraction and VERification Over Unstructured and Structured information","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.05707","arxiv_id":"2106.05707","authors":["Rami Aly","Zhijiang Guo","Michael Sejr Schlichtkrull","James Thorne","Andreas Vlachos","Christos Christodoulopoulos","Oana Cocarascu","Arpit Mittal"],"year":2021,"abstract":"Introduces a fact-verification dataset in which claims must be checked against evidence drawn from both Wikipedia sentences and table cells. Claims receive support, refutation, or insufficient-evidence labels, enabling empirical study of corroboration across heterogeneous evidence.","venue":"Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, Datasets and Benchmarks Track"}
{"title":"DialFact: A Benchmark for Fact-Checking in Dialogue","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.08222","arxiv_id":"2110.08222","doi":"10.18653/v1/2022.acl-long.263","authors":["Prakhar Gupta","Chien-Sheng Wu","Wenhao Liu","Caiming Xiong"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Introduces a benchmark for fact-checking claims made in multi-turn dialogue against external Wikipedia evidence. It evaluates claim detection, evidence retrieval, and verification into supported, refuted, or insufficient-evidence classes, providing a conversational setting in which a speaker's assertion is distinct from corroborating evidence.","venue":"Proceedings of the 60th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"AVeriTeC: A Dataset for Real-world Claim Verification with Evidence from the Web","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.13117","arxiv_id":"2305.13117","authors":["Michael Schlichtkrull","Zhijiang Guo","Andreas Vlachos"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Introduces a dataset of real-world claims from fact-checking organizations with web evidence represented as question-answer pairs and textual justifications. The annotation procedure is designed to obtain sufficient contemporaneous evidence while reducing temporal leakage, supporting verification of claims against independently gathered sources."}
{"title":"Fully Automated Fact Checking Using External Sources","url":"https://aclanthology.org/R17-1046/","authors":["Georgi Karadzhov","Preslav Nakov","Lluís Màrquez","Alberto Barrón-Cedeño","Ivan Koychev"],"year":2017,"abstract":"Presents an automated fact-checking framework that searches for external sources and combines semantic text representations with estimates of source reliability. The method is evaluated on rumor detection and community question-answering fact-checking tasks.","venue":"Proceedings of the International Conference Recent Advances in Natural Language Processing, RANLP 2017"}
{"title":"ProvenanceGuard: Source-Aware Factuality Verification for MCP-Based LLM Agents","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18037","arxiv_id":"2606.18037","authors":["Ander Alvarez","Santhiya Rajan","Samuel Mugel","Román Orús"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-06-16","abstract":"Presents a source-aware verification system that decomposes generated answers into atomic claims, routes each claim to source-specific evidence, and verifies both factual support and attribution. The evaluation uses agent traces with stable source and tool identifiers and includes controlled source-attribution perturbations, directly testing whether evidence comes from the claimed source."}
{"title":"How and Why is An Answer (Still) Correct? Maintaining Provenance in Dynamic Knowledge Graphs","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.14864","arxiv_id":"2007.14864","authors":["Garima Gaur","Arnab Bhattacharya","Srikanta Bedathur"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Studies maintenance of query-result provenance as knowledge graphs change through insertions and deletions. The proposed method preserves provenance expressions that explain why an answer remains valid or becomes affected by graph updates, providing a transferable model for non-destructive historical evidence lineage."}
{"title":"Online maintenance of evolving knowledge graphs with RDFS-based saturation and why-provenance support","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.websem.2023.100796","doi":"10.1016/j.websem.2023.100796","authors":["Khalid Belhajjame","Mohamed-Yassine Mejri"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Presents an incremental method for maintaining inferred knowledge and why-provenance as an RDF knowledge graph evolves. The approach retains derivational explanations for asserted and inferred facts while processing changes, providing a transferable provenance mechanism once source statements have already been interpreted.","venue":"Journal of Web Semantics"}
{"title":"Belief Revision: The Adaptability of Large Language Models Reasoning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.19764","arxiv_id":"2406.19764","doi":"10.18653/v1/2024.emnlp-main.586","authors":["Bryan Wilie","Samuel Cahyawijaya","Etsuko Ishii","Junxian He","Pascale Fung"],"year":2024,"abstract":"Introduces a benchmark for evaluating whether language models revise conclusions when sequentially supplied premises or evidence change. It tests delta reasoning and belief updating across evolving information rather than evaluating only a single static inference instance.","venue":"Proceedings of the 2024 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing"}
===END llm_candidates.jsonl===
